<BlogPost slug="chronic-alcoholism-signs-and-treatment" toc={toc}>
  <p>In society, drinking alcohol is normalized: it's sometimes even encouraged and celebrated. In fact, more than 80% of males and nearly 78% of females aged 12 and older report drinking alcohol at least once in their lives. But when is consuming alcohol a problem, and even life-threatening?</p>
  <p><b>Chronic alcoholism</b> is a common way of referring to an Alcohol Use Disorder (AUD), which is when a person drinks alcohol excessively, to the point of being physically dependent on it, and to the detriment of their health, relationships, and daily functioning. Someone who has an AUD may be called a chronic alcoholic.</p>
  <p>Let's dive deeper into what chronic alcoholism is, how to recognize its signs, and what treatment can support someone struggling.</p>

  <h2 id="what-is">What is chronic alcoholism?</h2>
  <p>Chronic alcoholism is a condition where a person cannot stop drinking regularly, despite the negative effect on their health and livelihood. It's an addiction also known as alcoholism. The "chronic" part of chronic alcoholism implies that the person has been consuming alcohol for a long amount of time, which could mean they have a severe form of the disorder.</p>
  <p>In the US, 1 in 10 people report having an alcohol use disorder. There aren't exact numbers that indicate when someone has developed the disorder, but the National Institute on Alcohol Abuse and Alcoholism qualifies heavy drinking as:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Women:</b> 4 or more drinks per day, or 8 or more drinks per week</li>
    <li><b>Men:</b> 5 or more drinks per day, or 15 or more drinks per week</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="signs">What are signs of chronic alcoholism?</h2>
  <p>Signs of chronic alcoholism are varied and span physical, behavioral, and mental symptoms. They include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Cravings for alcohol</li>
    <li>Continuing to drink even when it disrupts work, relationships, and daily functioning</li>
    <li>Drinking more than intended, for longer than planned</li>
    <li>Spending a significant amount of time recovering from hangovers caused by drinking</li>
    <li>Missing personal and professional responsibilities due to drinking</li>
    <li>Giving up hobbies and social plans to drink instead</li>
    <li>Drinking alcohol in dangerous situations, such as when driving</li>
    <li>Drinking even when it worsens an existing health condition</li>
    <li>Needing to consume more alcohol to achieve the same effects as before</li>
    <li>Experiencing withdrawal symptoms when stopping drinking alcohol</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Research suggests that around half of people with an AUD will develop withdrawal symptoms when they stop or reduce how much they drink. Withdrawal symptoms appear because alcohol is a depressant that slows the central nervous system.</p>
  <p>Over time, the body becomes used to alcohol, and when it's removed or significantly reduced, internal systems go into overdrive and lead to withdrawal symptoms such as:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tremors</li>
    <li>Insomnia</li>
    <li>Anxiety</li>
    <li>Hypervigilance</li>
    <li>Loss of appetite</li>
    <li>Vomiting</li>
    <li>Sweating</li>
  </ul>
  <p><b>Alcohol Withdrawal Delirium (AWD)</b>, commonly known as Delirium Tremens (DTs), and seizures are more severe withdrawal symptoms that can be life-threatening. Both symptoms affect around 1% of the population with chronic alcoholism.</p>
  <p>People who have consumed large amounts of alcohol over a long period are more likely to experience severe withdrawal symptoms for a longer time. In extreme cases, withdrawal has been reported to last for up to two weeks. That is why a medically supervised <a href="/programs/medical-detox/">alcohol detox</a> is so important.</p>

