  <h2 id="brain">The effects of chronic alcoholism on the brain</h2>
  <p>Abusing alcohol takes a toll on the brain; it actually causes changes to how the brain looks and works. Chronic alcoholism reduces the size of neurons, which are the messenger cells in the brain. It also impairs the areas of the brain that are responsible for memory recall, sleep regulation, speech, balance, and decision-making. As a result, chronic alcoholics are more likely to be injured, fall behind at work, and damage their relationships.</p>
  <p>These changes also contribute to chronic alcoholic behaviors. Heavier, more frequent drinking causes brain changes that make stopping even harder.</p>

  <h2 id="risk">Who is at risk of chronic alcoholism?</h2>
  <p>Anyone can develop an AUD, however, there are certain groups and factors that increase the odds of becoming addicted to alcohol. These include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A family member with chronic alcoholism</li>
    <li>A family member or partner with another substance use disorder</li>
    <li>A mental health disorder such as depression or anxiety</li>
    <li>Drinking or being exposed to drinking from a young age</li>
    <li>High stress or trauma</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Mental health disorders commonly co-occur with chronic alcoholism. They can drive excessive drinking, even though excessive drinking exacerbates mental health disorders. Depression, anxiety, and other psychiatric disorders are more prevalent among people with an AUD compared to the general population. When both are present, <a href="/programs/dual-diagnosis-treatment/">dual diagnosis treatment</a> addresses them together.</p>

  <h2 id="treatment">Is there treatment for chronic alcoholism?</h2>
  <p>Yes, there is effective and readily available treatment for chronic alcoholism. At Regain Hope Detox, we have a medically supervised detox program to safely withdraw from alcohol. After detox, our <a href="/programs/residential-treatment/">residential program</a> supports abstinence and addresses the deeper causes of addiction.</p>
  <p>Care typically consists of:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Evidence-based therapies, such as Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)</li>
    <li><a href="/programs/medication-assisted-treatment/">Medication-Assisted Treatment (MAT)</a></li>
    <li>Holistic activities such as yoga, music therapy, and equine therapy</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Research confirms that structured treatment reduces symptoms, improves mental and physical health, and lowers the risk of relapse for people with chronic alcoholism. Our team has seen first-hand how treatment facilitates motivation, community-building, and goal-setting. All these qualities fuel successful, sustained recovery.</p>

